```ts
export function compareVersions(a: Version, b: Version): number {
  const cmp = <T>(x: T | null, y: T | null, order: (x: T, y: T) => number): number => {
    if (x === null && y === null) return 0
    if (x === null) return 1 // null sorts last
    if (y === null) return -1
    return order(x, y)
  }

  return (
    cmp(a.variant, b.variant, (x, y) => x.localeCompare(y)) ||
    (a.major - b.major) ||
    cmp(a.minor, b.minor, (x, y) => x - y) ||
    cmp(a.patch, b.patch, (x, y) => x - y) ||
    cmp(a.prerelease, b.prerelease, (x, y) => x.localeCompare(y)) ||
    cmp(a.build, b.build, (x, y) => x.localeCompare(y))
  )
}
```

- Noticed all five nullable fields share one rule ("null sorts last") and factored it into a single local `cmp` helper.
- Replaced each field's pair of `if` branches with one `cmp(...)` call.
- Chained the six field comparisons with `||`, relying on any nonzero (including negative) number being truthy in JS, matching the original short-circuit-on-first-difference behavior.
- Kept `major` (never null) as a direct subtraction, unchanged.

